WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.454
Idea. A tent pole stands straight up from the middle of the floor. A rope runs from the top of the pole to a corner. The pole, the floor and the rope make a right-angled triangle.

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:26.397
A solid is a three dimensional shape. Inside it you can always find a flat right-angled triangle, and Pythagoras works in it. A rectangle with sides a and b has a diagonal of root of a squared plus b squared.

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:38.681
In a right pyramid, the top point, called the vertex, is directly above the centre of the base. That is where the two diagonals cross. The centre is halfway along each diagonal.

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:46.447
So this type asks for a length in a solid. Find a right-angled triangle, draw it flat, and use Pythagoras.

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:59.121
Method. Step one: find a right-angled triangle that contains the length you want, and draw it flat. Step two: work out the base lengths first, like half the base diagonal.

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:10.190
Step three: use Pythagoras, or a trig ratio if an angle is given. Step four: keep surds exact. A rounded value cannot reach a printed answer.

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:14.387
The trap is using the whole diagonal where half is needed.

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:34.259
Worked example. November twenty twenty five. Figure four shows a right pyramid A B C D E. The base is a horizontal square of side six centimetres. The sloping edges are all twelve centimetres. The height is h. Show that h is three root fourteen. Three marks.

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:43.901
Step one: the base diagonal. A C is the square root of six squared plus six squared. That is root seventy two, which is six root two.

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:56.159
Step two: let O be the centre. The vertex is above O, so E O is vertical. A O is half of A C, which is three root two. Triangle E O A is right-angled at O.

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:08.676
Step three: Pythagoras in triangle E O A. h squared is twelve squared minus eighteen. That is one hundred and twenty six. Then h is root one hundred and twenty six.

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:20.203
Step four: one hundred and twenty six is nine times fourteen. So root one hundred and twenty six is three root fourteen. That is the answer we were asked to show.

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:32.675
A tempting route uses the whole diagonal. Then h squared is one hundred and forty four minus seventy two, which is seventy two. That gives six root two, not three root fourteen.

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:40.481
So h is three root fourteen. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:52.831
Common mistake. A common mistake is treating the sloping edge as the height. The sloping edge is the longest side of the triangle. So the height must be shorter than twelve.

00:02:53.960 --> 00:03:11.581
Your turn. Now you try, from November twenty twenty. A rectangle based pyramid has height fourteen centimetres. The base is twelve by sixteen. Find the length of a sloping edge, to three significant figures. Write your answer, then choose Continue to Answer.

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:22.897
Answer. The base diagonal is the square root of twelve squared plus sixteen squared, which is twenty. M is the centre, so A M is half of that: ten.

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:35.169
Triangle E M A is right-angled at M. E A squared is ten squared plus fourteen squared, which is two hundred and ninety six. E A is seventeen point two centimetres.

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:43.347
The common wrong answer uses the whole diagonal, twenty, giving twenty four point four. Use half the diagonal.

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:48.535
Remember: right-angled triangle, base length first, then Pythagoras.
